      "mimeType": "text/markdown",
      "text": "# Evidence and reporting\n\nUse this reference only for the optional ten-question discovery sample.\n\n## Classify each searched question\n\nEvery completed observation needs at least one public source URL cited by the researched answer.\n\n| Classification | Use only when |\n| --- | --- |\n| `not_seen` | Neither the answer nor its cited sources identifies the target business. Include the sources that did appear; do not supply a target evidence URL. |\n| `source_only` | A cited source identifies the target, but the answer itself does not name it. |\n| `mentioned` | The answer names the target but does not present it as a suitable choice for the need. |\n| `recommended` | The answer presents the target as a suitable option for that particular need. |\n\nFor `source_only`, `mentioned` or `recommended`, set `target_evidence_url` to one of the URLs in `evidence_urls`. It must be a cited page that supports the appearance. A search-result snippet alone is not evidence.\n\nList another provider only when the answer or a cited source actually identifies it. Keep provider names neutral and do not infer quality, market share or competitive rank.\n\n## Report shape\n\nLead with the returned headline and exact completed counts. Then show:\n\n1. **Where it appeared** — up to three strongest customer questions and whether each was cited, mentioned or recommended.\n2. **Where it was absent** — up to three commercially relevant missed questions.\n3. **What to improve next** — up to three actions grounded in the observed pages and competing sources.\n\nFinish with the returned deeper-review invitation and a small limitations sentence. Avoid a large limitations section.\n\nUse precise language: “appeared,” “was mentioned,” “was recommended in this sample,” or “was not seen.” Never say the result proves ranking, future recommendations, customer demand, conversion or revenue.\n\n## Incomplete samples\n\nIf fewer than ten questions were completed, say so near the headline. Do not convert the result to a score or extrapolate the missing questions. A smaller sourced sample is preferable to ten unsupported classifications.\n"
